{"canonical_record":{"canonical_payload_sha256":"08666ad7be37294e8ba645733afd76b767a654909130aa085009ac6a8edd55db","full_review_record_id":"fullrev_33ed5dc2839e0d8939cc031137f1e1543818441ecd3032a83ff50713242f646a","overall_assessment":"The paper presents a novel methodological framework, Lie-access memory, that redefines neural memory access through the lens of Lie group actions. This approach offers a distinct conceptual alternative to existing soft-memory mechanisms by explicitly enforcing group-theoretic properties like invertibility and identity, which the authors argue are missing in prior differentiable memory models. The contribution is primarily methodological, supported by qualitative analyses that reveal how the model internalizes discrete data structures within continuous manifolds.","paper_id":"Byiy-Pqlx","paper_identity_sha256":"f6e0d951823634b933891253f40ba77d7721e09de18e90cd01cc693bac19f294","prompt_identity_sha256":"b266cc08361bc42faa73c87e13808649acaa6729b05dd23ddf795fb89c840cbf","raw_response_sha256":"b39dcdb5e2cced9afd706119f0ff5f3c1fe67ef35da6e38874f1f21a7eda10f1","request_identity_sha256":"797c543b37ad36078a1b029fdde9b38e0ebc1f374b339d4aaed803004e17cd6e","reviewer_identity_sha256":"c1cd7c7fdd4a2cbeb45c2f21af982ad0f30bffae8ae148e3eba36479685cf360","role":"ORIG","schema_version":"agentreview-full-review-corpus-v4","structural_context_sha256":"32a4e9bbb0bfc456d08bacddaf18d0688b66dfa3572ba8d01071cfe916781e27","subdimension_sections":[{"paper_anchor_ids":["SRC-004"],"review_text":"The paper does not introduce a new problem, task, or dataset. Instead, it addresses the existing challenge of algorithmic learning with external memory by proposing a new formalism for memory access. The research question is framed as a methodological improvement to existing neural memory architectures rather than the definition of a novel research problem.","subdimension_id":"ORIG-PROB"},{"paper_anchor_ids":["SRC-004","SRC-012","SRC-013","SRC-014"],"review_text":"The paper introduces a genuinely new algorithmic approach called Lie-access memory. It replaces the standard soft-tape or attention-based head movement with a continuous head moved by Lie group actions (such as shifts or rotations) on a key-space manifold. This is a conceptual advance over previous differentiable memories, as it explicitly incorporates group axioms (identity, invertibility, and associativity) into the memory access mechanism, providing a structured alternative to the probabilistic or convolutional head movements found in prior work.","subdimension_id":"ORIG-MTH"},{"paper_anchor_ids":["SRC-016","SRC-031","SRC-032"],"review_text":"The paper provides insightful qualitative analysis of the learned memory structures. It demonstrates that the LANTM and SLANTM models learn to represent discrete data structures, such as cyclic lists and priority-ordered sequences, within continuous key spaces. The analysis of the SLANTM model on the repeat copy task, where the model implements a cyclic list on a sphere, offers a new perspective on how neural networks can internalize algorithmic logic through geometric constraints.","subdimension_id":"ORIG-ANL"},{"paper_anchor_ids":["SRC-023","SRC-025"],"review_text":"The experimental setup is not a primary contribution of the paper. The authors utilize a series of standard algorithmic tasks (copy, reverse, bigram flip, etc.) that are largely derived from or similar to those used in previous work on neural memory, such as Grefenstette et al. (2015). While the paper includes some additional tasks like repeat copy and priority sort, the overall evaluation protocol is consistent with existing benchmarks in the field rather than introducing a new dataset or evaluation standard.","subdimension_id":"ORIG-EXP"},{"paper_anchor_ids":["SRC-014","SRC-019"],"review_text":"The paper combines the LSTM controller with Lie group actions and a distance-based soft weighting scheme. The rationale for this combination is well-articulated: the authors argue that Lie groups provide a natural generalization of discrete memory structures that maintains differentiability while satisfying group axioms. This combination is presented as a material contribution that yields a more robust relative indexing scheme compared to existing soft-memory techniques.","subdimension_id":"ORIG-COM"},{"paper_anchor_ids":["SRC-012"],"review_text":"The paper does not focus on negative results or critical assessments of existing methods as a primary contribution. While it notes that existing soft-tape machines do not satisfy invertibility, this observation serves to motivate the proposed Lie-access framework rather than presenting a systematic failure analysis or a critical evaluation of the field's assumptions.","subdimension_id":"ORIG-NEG"}]},"disposition":"CANONICAL_FULL_REVIEW_COMMITTED","item_commit_sha256":"930ccda6e3f18ef87e4b6075eced049eed9604c8d6b6cd5de7545f1662d6764c","paper_id":"Byiy-Pqlx","paper_ordinal":76,"raw_attempts":[{"assistant_content_base64":"","assistant_content_sha256":"b39dcdb5e2cced9afd706119f0ff5f3c1fe67ef35da6e38874f1f21a7eda10f1","attempt_index":0,"error":"","finish_reason":"stop","paper_id":"Byiy-Pqlx","raw_provider_response_base64":"","raw_provider_response_sha256":"86e8167762dfb3ad59cd6060a18f4d6d634ec8da2e5d984a715af15be664bf74","request_identity_sha256":"797c543b37ad36078a1b029fdde9b38e0ebc1f374b339d4aaed803004e17cd6e","role":"ORIG","schema_version":"no-memory-full-review-corpus-build-v5","usage":{"completion_tokens":970,"prompt_tokens":19866,"prompt_tokens_details":null,"total_tokens":20836},"valid":true}],"request_identity_sha256":"797c543b37ad36078a1b029fdde9b38e0ebc1f374b339d4aaed803004e17cd6e","role":"ORIG","role_ordinal":3,"schema_version":"corpus-build-bootstrap-checkpoint-v4"}